AACDecode() bounds the per-element channel count against AAC_MAX_NCHANS,
not against the channel count the instance was configured with. For a
mono stream (raw block mode, AACSetRawBlockParams(nChans = 1)) a frame
that parses as a CPE therefore passes the check, and StereoProcess()
then reads coef[1] and the channel-1 scalefactors, which AllocateBuffers()
never allocated. The result is a heap read past the end of the mono
buffer inside StereoProcessGroup(), not an error return.

This is reachable from a network client: a malformed or mis-framed AAC
backchannel payload can parse as a CPE and take the process down.

Bound the element channel count by aacDecInfo->nChans as well, and check
that currBlockID indexes elementNumChans[] before using it. Invalid
frames now return ERR_AAC_CHANNEL_MAP / ERR_AAC_INVALID_FRAME and the
decoder instance stays usable.

--- a/src/libhelix-aac/aacdec.c
+++ b/src/libhelix-aac/aacdec.c
@@ -386,9 +386,13 @@
             return err;
         }
 
+        if (aacDecInfo->currBlockID < 0 ||
+            aacDecInfo->currBlockID >= NUM_ELEMENTS) {
+            return ERR_AAC_INVALID_FRAME;
+        }
         elementChans = elementNumChans[aacDecInfo->currBlockID];
-        if (baseChan + elementChans > AAC_MAX_NCHANS) {
-            return ERR_AAC_NCHANS_TOO_HIGH;
+        if (baseChan + elementChans > aacDecInfo->nChans) {
+            return ERR_AAC_CHANNEL_MAP;
         }
 
         /* noiseless decoder and dequantizer */
